<header><p class="st-kicker">kingdom · South Asia</p><h1 class="st-title">Indo-Greek Kingdom</h1><p class="st-meta">180 BCE – 10 CE (approx.) · capital Sagala</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Greek kingdoms in north-western India founded by Alexander's Bactrian heirs, ruled by kings like Menander who mixed Greek and Indian culture.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Around 180 BCE Demetrius I of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/greco-bactrian-kingdom">Greco-Bactrian Kingdom</a> crossed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hindu-kush">Hindu Kush</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kabul">Kabul</a> valley and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a>, taking advantage of the fall of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/maurya-empire">Maurya Empire</a>. His successors founded a series of Greek-ruled kingdoms in <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a> and the Punjab, often rivals of one another and of their Bactrian cousins.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Indo-Greek kings issued bilingual coins with Greek on one side and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prakrit">Prakrit</a> in Kharoshthi on the other, often showing Greek gods beside Indian symbols. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/menander-i">Menander I</a>, ruling from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sagala">Sagala</a>, raided deep into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ganges-plain">Ganges plain</a> and appears in the Buddhist text <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milindapanha">Milindapanha</a> as King Milinda, questioning the monk <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagasena">Nagasena</a>. The envoy <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/heliodorus-pillar">Heliodorus</a>, sent by King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antialcidas">Antialcidas</a>, set up a pillar to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vasudeva">Vasudeva</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vidisha">Vidisha</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Pressed by <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/indo-scythians">Indo-Scythians</a> and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/yuezhi">Yuezhi</a> from the north-west, the Indo-Greek kingdoms shrank during the 1st century BCE. The last known king, Strato II, ruled in the eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/punjab">Punjab</a> until around 10. Their artistic legacy fed into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gandhara-art">Greco-Buddhist art</a> of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-gandhara">Gandhara</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/indo-greek-kingdom.jpg" alt="Indo-Greek Kingdom" width="320" height="161"><figcaption>Image: Classical Numismatic Group · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Coin_of_Hippostratos.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>180 BCE – 10 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/2nd-century-bce">2nd century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/1st-century">1st century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>kingdom</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>bureaucratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Sagala</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Demetrius I, Menander I, Antialcidas</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion, Buddhism, Hinduism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Greek, Gandhari Prakrit</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/pakistan">Pakistan</a>, <a href="/countries/afghanistan">Afghanistan</a>, <a href="/countries/india">India</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Indo-Greeks, Yavana kingdoms, Graeco-Indian Kingdom</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-85&amp;focus=indo-greek-kingdom" aria-label="Indo-Greek Kingdom on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="6484 1036 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Indo-Greek Kingdom lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M6804 1292L6680 1109M6804 1292L6948 1391"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M6680 1109h0M6948 1391h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M6804 1292h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M6804 1292h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=-85&amp;focus=indo-greek-kingdom">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
